WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.815
Avery realizes that leading a useful meeting takes more than preparing an agenda.

2
00:00:04.955 --> 00:00:13.039
Instead of deciding, I am bad at management, Avery identifies a skill to practice: drawing out concerns before choosing a solution.

3
00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:17.655
A specific development need is easier to act on than a label about your worth.

4
00:00:17.795 --> 00:00:22.759
The learning loop shows a skill gap, a practice opportunity, feedback, and another attempt.

5
00:00:22.899 --> 00:00:33.386
Avery might observe an experienced supervisor, lead a short planning conversation, then ask whether participants understood the decision and felt their information was considered.

6
00:00:33.526 --> 00:00:36.089
A mentor can help interpret that feedback.

7
00:00:36.229 --> 00:00:41.014
Chapter twenty-one also emphasizes networking and continuing education.

8
00:00:41.154 --> 00:00:45.782
These can include colleagues, professional reading, workshops, and project leadership.

9
00:00:45.922 --> 00:00:51.475
Students can begin through a group project, a campus job, or a volunteer responsibility.

10
00:00:51.615 --> 00:00:56.649
Individuals have a role in their growth, and organizations have a role in making it possible.

11
00:00:56.789 --> 00:01:01.376
Time, funding, accessible training, and fair access to opportunities matter.

12
00:01:01.516 --> 00:01:04.568
Progress is not determined by confidence alone.

13
00:01:04.708 --> 00:01:10.749
Asking for help and requesting realistic support are professional actions, not admissions of failure.
